# Normalise everything to LF in the repository.
#
# Without this, a Windows checkout commits CRLF and every Linux contributor and CI run sees
# a whole-file diff on files nobody touched. It also keeps the tree consistent with
# .editorconfig, which already declares end_of_line = lf.
#
# This matters more than usual here: the domain prompt's SHA-256 appears in every execution
# trace, and a line-ending rewrite would silently change that hash without changing a word.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Data files stay LF regardless of platform.
*.py    text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.toml  text eol=lf

# Windows-only scripts keep CRLF so they run when checked out there.
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.ps1   text eol=crlf

# Binary: never diffed, never normalised.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.webp  binary
*.whl   binary

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# MUST BE LAST: in .gitattributes the last matching rule wins, and `*.md text eol=lf` above
# would otherwise normalise this file.
#
# The domain prompt is a byte-exact, user-managed artifact whose SHA-256 is recorded in
# every execution trace. Normalising its line endings would change that hash without
# changing a single word, silently invalidating every prior trace and breaking the contract
# test that asserts the loaded bytes equal the bytes on disk.
prompts/domain/system-prompt.md -text -diff
